The patent describes a diesel-engine exhaust treatment system designed to coordinate particulate-filter regeneration with control of the exhaust’s NO₂-to-total-NOx ratio.

An upstream oxidation catalyst converts part of the engine-out NO into NO₂. Sensors and an electronic controller monitor operating conditions and regulate this conversion—potentially through catalyst temperature, exhaust routing, or engine settings—so that enough NO₂ is available to oxidize trapped soot without producing excessive NO₂ emissions.

Downstream, a catalytic particulate filter captures soot. A fuel- or exhaust-borne additive, typically containing a catalytic metal, is introduced to lower the soot’s combustion temperature and assist filter regeneration. The combination of additive-catalyzed soot and controlled NO₂ enables regeneration at lower exhaust temperatures, reducing reliance on high-temperature active regeneration.

Overall, the invention aims to:

- maintain a desired NO₂/NOx ratio;
- improve passive and low-temperature filter regeneration;
- reduce fuel consumption and thermal stress associated with forced regeneration;
- prevent excessive tailpipe NO₂; and
- coordinate particulate and NOx treatment across changing engine conditions.

### Exemplary Patent-Style Method

A method for treating exhaust gas produced by an internal-combustion engine, the method comprising:

1. **oxidizing exhaust constituents** by directing the exhaust gas through an oxidation catalyst positioned downstream of the internal-combustion engine, the oxidation catalyst being configured to oxidize at least one of hydrocarbons, carbon monoxide, and nitric oxide, thereby increasing a concentration of nitrogen dioxide in the exhaust gas;

2. **determining an operating condition** of the internal-combustion engine and an exhaust-aftertreatment system using one or more sensor signals indicative of at least one of engine speed, engine load, air-to-fuel ratio, exhaust temperature, exhaust mass flow, particulate-filter pressure differential, nitrogen-oxide concentration, oxygen concentration, reductant concentration, and ammonia slip;

3. **controlling at least one engine operating parameter in closed-loop fashion**, by means of an electronic controller responsive to the one or more sensor signals, to establish or maintain a target exhaust condition suitable for particulate oxidation and catalytic reduction of nitrogen oxides, wherein the at least one engine operating parameter comprises one or more of fuel-injection timing, fuel-injection quantity, exhaust-gas recirculation rate, intake-air throttling, boost pressure, valve timing, and post-injection quantity;

4. **introducing a reductant** into the exhaust gas downstream of the oxidation catalyst and upstream of a particulate filter, at a dosing rate determined by the electronic controller as a function of an estimated or measured nitrogen-oxide mass flow, a temperature of the particulate filter, an estimated reductant-storage state, and a permissible reductant-slip limit;

5. **mixing or decomposing the reductant** in the exhaust gas to provide a nitrogen-containing reducing species, optionally comprising ammonia generated from an aqueous urea solution; and

6. **passing the reductant-containing exhaust gas through the particulate filter**, the particulate filter comprising a porous filter substrate carrying a selective catalytic reduction catalyst, such that:
   - particulate matter is retained by the porous filter substrate;
   - at least a portion of the retained particulate matter is oxidized using oxygen and/or nitrogen dioxide; and
   - at least a portion of the nitrogen oxides is catalytically reduced, in the presence of the reducing species, to molecular nitrogen,

wherein the electronic controller cooperatively regulates the at least one engine operating parameter and the dosing rate to maintain the particulate filter within a predetermined operating region in which oxidation of retained particulate matter and reduction of nitrogen oxides occur concurrently.

In certain embodiments, the method further comprises determining a soot-loading value from the differential pressure across the particulate filter, an engine-out particulate model, or both, and modifying at least one of exhaust temperature, nitrogen-dioxide-to-nitrogen-oxide ratio, oxygen concentration, and reductant dosing in response to the soot-loading value.

In certain embodiments, closed-loop control comprises comparing a measured downstream nitrogen-oxide concentration with a target nitrogen-oxide concentration and adjusting the reductant dosing rate based on an error therebetween, while limiting the dosing rate according to an estimated ammonia-storage capacity of the selective catalytic reduction catalyst.

In certain embodiments, the controller initiates an active regeneration mode when the soot-loading value exceeds a threshold, the active regeneration mode comprising increasing exhaust-gas temperature through adjustment of one or more engine operating parameters, while continuing reductant dosing at a rate selected to provide nitrogen-oxide conversion without exceeding a predetermined ammonia-slip threshold.

Accordingly, the oxidation catalyst, closed-loop engine control, controlled reductant introduction, and catalytically coated particulate filter operate as an integrated exhaust-treatment system to provide simultaneous particulate-matter removal and nitrogen-oxide reduction.
